WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.889
Testing asks whether a program does what you expected.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:08.786
Debugging is the investigation that follows when it does not.

00:00:08.786 --> 00:00:15.519
With an assistant, a wrong final answer may have started much earlier than the sentence you can see.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:18.489
A trace is like a receipt for the work.

00:00:18.489 --> 00:00:24.753
It can show which tool was requested, what information was sent to it, and what came back.

00:00:24.753 --> 00:00:26.915
Read those steps in order.

00:00:26.915 --> 00:00:29.983
Pick one failing request and repeat it.

00:00:29.983 --> 00:00:33.646
Compare expected with actual one step at a time.

00:00:33.646 --> 00:00:41.549
If an earlier lookup was already wrong, editing the final wording alone cannot recover the missing facts.

00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:44.232
Here is the trace of a healthy run.

00:00:44.232 --> 00:00:48.356
Step one: Scout calls get order with Ana's order number.

00:00:48.356 --> 00:00:52.258
The result comes back: shipped, with a tracking number.

00:00:52.258 --> 00:00:57.819
Step two: Scout calls track parcel, using the tracking number it just found.

00:00:57.819 --> 00:01:03.218
That result gives the arrival day, Thursday, which goes into the reply.

00:01:03.218 --> 00:01:08.354
Read a trace like a story: each step should follow from the one before.

00:01:08.354 --> 00:01:15.345
And when the reply and the trace disagree, trust the trace: it shows what Scout actually did.

00:01:15.345 --> 00:01:21.895
Grace asks whether she can return her boots and gives the email address used for her purchase.

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:24.494
Scout gives a reassuring answer.

00:01:24.494 --> 00:01:28.509
Before judging that sentence, ask what it would need to know.

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:35.561
The return window depends on the delivery date, so Scout first has to find Grace’s order.

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:37.296
Open the trace.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:44.116
The order tool accepts an order identifier in one field and an email address in another.

00:01:44.116 --> 00:01:48.099
Scout put Grace’s email into the order-number field.

00:01:48.099 --> 00:01:50.066
The lookup found nothing.

00:01:50.066 --> 00:01:54.124
The next mistake was answering as if it had found the order.

00:01:54.124 --> 00:01:57.912
The first wrong step is the misplaced email.

00:01:57.912 --> 00:02:00.766
Start at the last sentence Grace would see.

00:02:00.766 --> 00:02:05.602
It might sound polite even when the order lookup underneath it failed.

00:02:05.602 --> 00:02:13.400
To debug this case, work backwards until you find the first place where the evidence differs from the expectation.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.969
The tool name is correct, so checking its description again will not fix this run.

00:02:18.969 --> 00:02:22.452
Read the input fields beside the saved request.

00:02:22.452 --> 00:02:28.469
An email in the order-number field gives you a specific, reproducible mismatch.

00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:32.132
Correct the field and use the same message again.

00:02:32.132 --> 00:02:37.948
Now check the returned delivery date against the return rule before trusting the draft.

00:02:37.948 --> 00:02:41.013
A successful tool call fixes the lookup.

00:02:41.013 --> 00:02:45.514
A correct customer answer still requires a second check.

00:02:45.514 --> 00:02:48.815
Most agent bugs fall into four families.

00:02:48.815 --> 00:02:56.742
Wrong tool: Scout searches the policy when it should look up the order, usually because two descriptions overlap.

00:02:56.742 --> 00:03:03.411
Wrong arguments: the right tool with the wrong input, like an email where an order number should be.

00:03:03.411 --> 00:03:08.748
Ignored error: a tool fails, and Scout answers confidently anyway.

00:03:08.748 --> 00:03:14.672
Looping: Scout calls the same tool again and again, hoping for a different result.

00:03:14.672 --> 00:03:18.452
Once you can name the bug, the fix is usually clear.

00:03:18.452 --> 00:03:25.119
Each family leaves its own fingerprint in the trace, so with practice you'll spot them at a glance.

00:03:25.119 --> 00:03:29.082
Correct the field and repeat Grace’s same request.

00:03:29.082 --> 00:03:35.782
Check that the lookup now finds the matching order, then compare the delivery date with the return rule.

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:39.788
Finally repeat a request that supplies an order number.

00:03:39.788 --> 00:03:47.103
You have evidence of a fix when Grace’s case improves and the previously working case still works.
